@* One badge answering "is this a service or a job?", shared by every surface that lists both.

   Centralized rather than written per call site because these surfaces had already drifted: the
   Services & Jobs dialog showed a colored chip while the Applications tab showed bare text, so the
   same distinction looked like two different things depending on where you read it.

   The icons carry the actual semantic difference, which the words alone don't make obvious to
   someone new: a service runs CONTINUOUSLY (Autorenew — the looping arrows), a job runs ON A
   SCHEDULE and is idle in between (Schedule — a clock). That distinction is exactly the one behind
   the Running/Enabled state split elsewhere in this table, so the icon reinforces it rather than
   introducing a new idea. *@

@if (Icon is null)
{
    @* Not a service or a job — e.g. the "Policy conflict" pseudo-row's em dash. A badge would imply
       a real instance kind that this row doesn't have. *@
    <span>@Type</span>
}
else
{
    <MudChip T="string" Size="Size.Small" Variant="Variant.Outlined" Icon="@Icon" Color="@Color" Style="@Style">@Type</MudChip>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>"Service" or "Job". Anything else renders as plain text — see above.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Type { get; set; } = "";

    private string? Icon => Type switch
    {
        "Service" => Icons.Material.Filled.Autorenew,
        "Job" => Icons.Material.Filled.Schedule,
        _ => null,
    };

    // Service takes Primary (the IronCanary blue); a job takes Info's lighter blue-cyan. Both are
    // deliberately not Secondary, which is the harsh pink in this palette, and job moved off Tertiary's
    // mint because it sat too close to the green "Enabled" state chip three columns along.
    private Color Color => Type == "Service" ? MudBlazor.Color.Primary : MudBlazor.Color.Info;

    /// <summary>
    /// MudBlazor's Color enum only exposes the base swatch — there is no Color.InfoLighten — so the
    /// lighter blue-cyan has to come from the palette's own CSS variable. Reading the variable rather
    /// than hardcoding rgb(92,173,255) keeps the badge tied to the theme: restyle Info in MainLayout and
    /// this follows automatically.
    ///
    /// Both properties are needed because the chip is Outlined: `color` paints the label and (via
    /// currentColor on the SVG) the icon, `border-color` paints the ring. Inline style beats MudChip's
    /// own class rules on specificity.
    ///
    /// Safe against the theme's dark ground because this portal is dark-only (MainLayout hardcodes
    /// IsDarkMode="true"); a light mint on a light surface would be the risky direction, and can't
    /// arise here.
    /// </summary>
    private string? Style => Type == "Job"
        ? "color: var(--mud-palette-info-lighten); border-color: var(--mud-palette-info-lighten);"
        : null;
}
